><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250605141641+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250605141643+10'00'" name="8eed95ca-64db-4c56-a70c-0a924de3b7e3" page="6" coords="330.971985,441.864105,575.971741,441.864105,330.971985,430.834167,575.971741,430.834167,313.000793,430.865997,575.926514,430.865997,313.000793,419.836060,575.926514,419.836060,313.000793,419.867889,575.964478,419.867889,313.000793,408.837952,575.964478,408.837952,313.000793,408.869781,575.979980,408.869781,313.000793,397.839844,575.979980,397.839844,313.000793,397.871674,575.921509,397.871674,313.000793,386.841736,575.921509,386.841736,313.000793,386.873566,575.941101,386.873566,313.000793,375.843628,575.941101,375.843628,313.000793,375.875458,575.961304,375.875458,313.000793,364.845520,575.961304,364.845520,313.000793,364.877350,575.973755,364.877350,313.000793,353.847412,575.973755,353.847412,313.000793,353.879242,576.013367,353.879242,313.000793,342.849304,576.013367,342.849304,313.000793,342.881134,575.949768,342.881134,313.000793,331.851196,575.949768,331.851196,313.000793,331.883026,576.012573,331.883026,313.000793,320.853088,576.012573,320.853088,313.000793,320.884918,443.345337,320.884918,313.000793,309.854980,443.345337,309.854980" rect="312.656097,309.510284,576.358032,442.208801" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>In conclusion, carefully selected anthropometric models could&#xD;be useful for estimating indices of lean tissue if other assessment&#xD;methods are inaccessible. Ideally, the model should be derived&#xD;from a similar population and from indirect measures of lean tissue.&#xD;It is also advocated that the practitioner uses landmarks consistent&#xD;with the original study to minimize noise. Compared with skinfold&#xD;anthropometric models assessed, the novel Sesbreno equation may&#xD;offer a suitable option for estimating absolute indices of lean tissue&#xD;to undertake broader assessments such as the interpretations of&#xD;RMR or energy availability. Future work should explore the&#xD;validity of the Sesbreno model across a broader range of physiques&#xD;common to athletic populations.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="6" rect="612.128723,113.762451,796.055359,441.688538"
/></highlight
